WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.542
[MUSIC]

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:13.562
Welcome, everybody, wherever you
are to this event sponsored by

00:00:13.562 --> 00:00:17.389
the cooperation society initiative,
CASI at Stanford GSB.

00:00:17.389 --> 00:00:22.725
My name is Anat Admati, and
I'm a faculty director of CASI.

00:00:22.725 --> 00:00:28.251
With CASI we seek to engage
the broader community here and elsewhere.

00:00:28.251 --> 00:00:32.956
On issues around governance, in business
and in government, which are essential for

00:00:32.956 --> 00:00:36.070
our market system to
deliver on its promise.

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:40.560
We want powerful people and
institutions to be accountable to society.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:45.490
I'm a professor of finance and today
is about finance and financial markets.

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:47.610
What does that have to do with CASI?

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.684
In our basic finance courses
which have taught for many years,

00:00:50.684 --> 00:00:55.300
we assert that the stock wise embodies
all the great things corporations can do.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:59.632
All the positive net present value
projects are basic teachings and

00:00:59.632 --> 00:01:04.960
their motto is that most academic study,
assume away the possibility.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.560
Have fraud and do not have hedge fund
investors who try to make money.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:16.126
Finding fraud and betting on price
going down when they do find it,

00:01:16.126 --> 00:01:20.500
but today we have that
counterexample right here.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:25.158
We're so lucky to be able to host
Tammy Khadia who will explain in

00:01:25.158 --> 00:01:29.970
further detail why her work is so
relevant for CASI.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:31.879
And moderating this event and

00:01:31.879 --> 00:01:36.790
introducing her father is one of our
amazing student leaders Crisco Baum.

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:40.179
Who actually is himself from
the financial sector and

00:01:40.179 --> 00:01:43.000
going back to the financial sector.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.500
So I'm sure it'll be
a fascinating discussion.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.893
And thank you all to our
leaders on the Romanies

00:01:49.893 --> 00:01:55.040
watching the chat,
the Q&amp;A for your questions.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:00.620
And thanks Loretta and the event team
at GSB for making this event possible.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.349
I let Chris take it over from here,
thank you very much.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:09.370
&gt;&gt; I thank you Anat and good afternoon to
everybody joining us here virtually today.

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:13.680
Glad to have you all here with us for
this very special session.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.060
By way of introduction,

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:19.480
I am a second year MBA student here at
the Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:24.580
And as Anat mentioned, I myself come
from the finance and investing world.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:28.558
With four years at JPMorgan for
the business school and

00:02:28.558 --> 00:02:33.762
joining Blackrock as an investment
professional on upon graduation.

00:02:33.762 --> 00:02:40.972
So without further ado, I am honored to
introduce Fahmi Quadir our guest of honor.

00:02:40.972 --> 00:02:45.779
Fahmi is the founder of the hedge fund
Stafkhet capital where she focuses on

00:02:45.779 --> 00:02:50.664
concentrated short positions driven by
in depth investigation of fraud and

00:02:50.664 --> 00:02:51.975
corporate crimes.

00:02:51.975 --> 00:02:55.407
For those in the room who may not be
familiar with the term short selling is

00:02:55.407 --> 00:02:58.556
when an investor like Fahmi borrows
shares of stock to sell today,

00:02:58.556 --> 00:03:01.410
with the expectation that
the stock price will fall.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:04.435
They will be able to buy those
shares back at a lower future price,

00:03:04.435 --> 00:03:06.880
thereby pocketing the difference.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.124
The potential losses
are theoretically infinite,

00:03:09.124 --> 00:03:12.020
as there's no limit to how
high a stock price can go.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:18.030
And in a bull market as we had the past
decade, stocks mostly tend to go up.

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:21.051
So Fahmi is here with us today to shed
a light on how she has persevered

00:03:21.051 --> 00:03:22.810
through such an environment.

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:25.810
And our work keeps companies
accountable at the end of the day.

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:30.500
So Fahmi, we'd love to start off by
getting your take on the state of play

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:33.930
today know with rising inflation
increasing shocks to the market,

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:35.310
geopolitical tension.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:37.392
Do you think we're in a materially
different spot from last year,

00:03:37.392 --> 00:03:38.070
what does that mean?

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.890
&gt;&gt; So, thank you Chris and
thank you Anat and

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:48.937
thank you to the Stanford community and to
CASI for hosting me virtually this year.

00:03:48.937 --> 00:03:54.166
I'm joining you all from London the latest
place that my work has taken me so

00:03:54.166 --> 00:03:56.250
it's evening here.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:04:03.245
And I'm hopeful that maybe next semester
I'll be able to join you guys on campus.

00:04:03.245 --> 00:04:08.476
[LAUGH] So to start, I think there's
little that's more insufferable

00:04:08.476 --> 00:04:14.190
than hearing single stock fundamental
investors talk economics.

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:17.900
So, [LAUGH] I'll do my best
to stay in my lane here.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:23.981
For as long as I've been in business,
I've heard managers very publicly,

00:04:23.981 --> 00:04:26.695
very boisterously complain about the FED,

00:04:26.695 --> 00:04:30.830
about negative interest rates
that quantitative easing.

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:34.390
And they've always blamed all of this for
their under performance.

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:36.517
At the same time,

00:04:36.517 --> 00:04:41.260
they've allowed this myth of the FED
put to become totally internalized.

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:44.970
It's a part of the collective
investment consciousness right now.

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:49.115
And this FED put has been used
to justify complete speculation,

00:04:49.115 --> 00:04:52.430
I think most normal people
would call it gambling.

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:58.290
These are behaviors that a few years ago
at least from my seat were things that

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:04.260
would be dismissed as day trading but it's
now become accepted investing practice.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:08.379
And we are seeing it especially
in liquid markets like crypto and

00:05:08.379 --> 00:05:10.640
like within equities.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:15.928
So this kind of gambling mentality has
almost become muscle memory for investors

00:05:15.928 --> 00:05:20.997
who once upon a time may have considered
themselves fundamental investors.

00:05:20.997 --> 00:05:22.757
So it's going to take a lot,

00:05:22.757 --> 00:05:27.010
a lot for this investors to unlearn
some of this bad behaviors.

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:33.406
So even if the economic realities
that you are talking about, wages,

00:05:33.406 --> 00:05:39.238
prices, UClean, even those we're
constantly exposed to these narratives,

00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:42.000
it's not really sinking in.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:46.076
Rather, it's sometimes what we see is
that the market is often driven by false

00:05:46.076 --> 00:05:47.380
narratives.

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:52.251
And one thing that I started hearing a lot
starting at the end of last year was

00:05:52.251 --> 00:05:55.930
that value is king and
that growth is dead.

00:05:55.930 --> 00:06:00.935
Okay, I can understand why people
might say this at first because

00:06:00.935 --> 00:06:06.990
of course we did see a sell off in the QQ
so the top 100 names in the NASDAQ.

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:12.270
So we saw a lot of the repricing and some
of these growth momentum driven names.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:16.940
But at the same time,
what does value really mean?

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:22.562
I believe that markets are generally
pretty efficient, so if something

00:06:22.562 --> 00:06:29.010
of company has been trading at a very
single digit multiple for many years.

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:32.870
I don't know if I would necessarily
call that cheap, it's probably garbage.

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:37.359
So, the thing that we
saw during the pandemic,

00:06:37.359 --> 00:06:44.431
were a lot of businesses that were on
their last leg that we're reaching for

00:06:44.431 --> 00:06:49.048
fraud for
example to extend their life-cycle.

00:06:49.048 --> 00:06:53.876
Suddenly had extra liquidity that
was completely unexpected, and

00:06:53.876 --> 00:06:57.866
over that period they were
able to buy additional time.

00:06:57.866 --> 00:07:02.193
Or buy additional time to continue
to defraud people for example.

00:07:02.193 --> 00:07:05.623
So we saw a lot of use these
consumer finance businesses for

00:07:05.623 --> 00:07:08.151
example we like to look at payday lenders.

00:07:08.151 --> 00:07:12.999
All of them got a new lease on life
because of all this liquidity, but

00:07:12.999 --> 00:07:18.120
the thing is these things were
always trading below book value.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:24.210
They were always trading as cheap
businesses on if you look at multiples.

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:25.240
Why is that?

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:29.925
[LAUGH] it's because these businesses
are a dependent on screwing

00:07:29.925 --> 00:07:32.100
over their customer.

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:36.839
So over time the business model
blows up especially when you're

00:07:36.839 --> 00:07:40.271
entering a tightening
cycle as far as credit.

00:07:40.271 --> 00:07:45.025
So this is one of the false
narratives that I've seen pop up in

00:07:45.025 --> 00:07:46.712
the past few months.

00:07:46.712 --> 00:07:51.438
Where people are considering only that
a business is generating cash but

00:07:51.438 --> 00:07:56.820
not the quality of that cash flow
which should be equally as important.

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:03.340
But another thing that I'm hearing
a lot is that someone is so stuck.

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:07.890
Is now down 50%, so
the bottom is in by the dip.

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:12.703
So what that tells me is that even
though the headlines are showing

00:08:12.703 --> 00:08:17.865
that we're all a lot more uncertain
about what the future looks like,

00:08:17.865 --> 00:08:22.417
people are still using share
price as a determining factor for

00:08:22.417 --> 00:08:25.640
their various investment decisions.

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:29.660
They're call on whether a business
is going to grow or fail.

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:33.740
It's, again, still driven by price,
driven by momentum.

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:38.306
So while some investors are starting
to get their mojo back,

00:08:38.306 --> 00:08:42.794
thinking that maybe we're
getting back to fundamentals.

00:08:42.794 --> 00:08:47.066
As someone who's sort of been
watching this play out, and

00:08:47.066 --> 00:08:52.211
is hyper exposed to prices that
are disconnected from fundamentals,

00:08:52.211 --> 00:08:57.985
as much as I'd like to say that we're
getting there, I don't know if we have.

00:08:57.985 --> 00:08:59.720
We're not there yet.

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:04.275
Again, we're trying to use this
false fundamental sprinkling of

00:09:04.275 --> 00:09:08.424
a narrative on top of something
that still hasn't changed,

00:09:08.424 --> 00:09:12.550
which is prices being
driven by these narratives.

00:09:12.550 --> 00:09:17.743
And sometimes these false narratives
is driven by sentiment and

00:09:17.743 --> 00:09:20.539
momentum, not by real analysis.

00:09:20.539 --> 00:09:26.427
So while it seems hopeful, and
if you just look at how the portfolio

00:09:26.427 --> 00:09:32.211
has performed over the last six
months compared to the past year,

00:09:32.211 --> 00:09:37.179
obviously, things seem like
they've gotten better.

00:09:37.179 --> 00:09:42.723
But it's never really satisfying when
things are moving for reasons other

00:09:42.723 --> 00:09:48.660
than the various pieces of information
we've picked out in our analysis.

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:53.210
&gt;&gt; So it's interesting Fahmi that you
mention this huge focus on narrative

00:09:53.210 --> 00:09:55.971
over analysis kind of
taking prevalence and

00:09:55.971 --> 00:10:00.539
determining how people are deciding
what to invest in the market today.

00:10:00.539 --> 00:10:02.512
And I think if you look back to last year,

00:10:02.512 --> 00:10:06.550
there's maybe no narrative that got
more press than the GameStop saga.

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:09.487
So I think one question that's on
our audience minds is what is your

00:10:09.487 --> 00:10:11.530
understanding of what happened there?

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:13.270
&gt;&gt; I don't know [LAUGH].

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:15.970
I get asked about this a lot.

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:22.178
But I think at base, while the media
is trying to create like a David,

00:10:22.178 --> 00:10:29.138
Goliath story, or talking about hedge
funds taking on excessive leverage,

00:10:29.138 --> 00:10:33.640
you're taking on these ridiculous bets.

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:35.950
That stuff has always existed.

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:38.364
Retail Investors have also always existed.

00:10:38.364 --> 00:10:45.393
They become more prevalent, and
they're more part of our focus as

00:10:45.393 --> 00:10:50.550
a society in the peak of the bull market,
right?

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:54.614
Because that's when people
start seeing advertisements for

00:10:54.614 --> 00:10:57.825
crypto while they're
watching the Superbowl.

00:10:57.825 --> 00:11:01.352
And then grandma might be
watching the Superbowl and

00:11:01.352 --> 00:11:05.451
saying I'm not making any money
holding my cash in the bank.

00:11:05.451 --> 00:11:10.380
Why don't I get into those high
yield crypto savings accounts?

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:17.497
So we're at that point in the cycle where
you kind of have this strange tension,

00:11:17.497 --> 00:11:24.630
where clearly things are maybe going to
not be as good as they've been, right?

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:29.185
We clearly had the pandemic, and
that has covered up a lot of bad behavior

00:11:29.185 --> 00:11:31.961
in some of the businesses
that we look like.

00:11:31.961 --> 00:11:36.890
It provided liquidity to businesses
that probably shouldn't have had it.

00:11:36.890 --> 00:11:43.852
But now we're going to see what is
the consequence of all of that?

00:11:43.852 --> 00:11:48.605
Have we seen peak growth
at least in the near term?

00:11:48.605 --> 00:11:53.769
And what does that mean for
businesses that were simply not growing,

00:11:53.769 --> 00:11:57.910
or were simply losing money for
the past decades?

00:11:57.910 --> 00:12:02.564
So as much as it feels
as though certain stocks

00:12:02.564 --> 00:12:07.094
have had these violent moves, in reality,

00:12:07.094 --> 00:12:11.881
I would say most people
are as bullish as ever.

00:12:11.881 --> 00:12:15.001
And there's a danger to this,

00:12:15.001 --> 00:12:19.921
because it's this mentality
that this idea that

00:12:19.921 --> 00:12:25.201
the stock market can never go down 50% or
80%,

00:12:25.201 --> 00:12:31.450
because it's almost like
an apocalyptic sort of situation.

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:36.252
People can't fathom the idea that prices
may correct, and multiples may change.

00:12:36.252 --> 00:12:39.954
And the way the economy has been growing,
it might slow down.

00:12:39.954 --> 00:12:44.521
It's like we've been trying
to delude ourselves into

00:12:44.521 --> 00:12:47.405
thinking this time is different.

00:12:47.405 --> 00:12:52.525
But it's rarely ever different, including
these situations like GameStop and

00:12:52.525 --> 00:12:55.095
AMC, and the rise of the retail trader.

00:12:55.095 --> 00:12:56.833
It always happens.

00:12:56.833 --> 00:13:02.855
It's really just everyone is going to
try to enjoy the party while it lasts,

00:13:02.855 --> 00:13:06.265
but who's going to be
the last one to leave?

00:13:09.081 --> 00:13:12.854
&gt;&gt; So I suppose with that focus
then on sort of narrative, and

00:13:12.854 --> 00:13:17.474
the rational factors taking a backseat,
that tends to drown out sort of

00:13:17.474 --> 00:13:20.863
the strength of a well
reasoned investment thesis.

00:13:20.863 --> 00:13:24.557
And I'm wondering then does that change
how you think about the role that you play

00:13:24.557 --> 00:13:25.275
in the market?

00:13:27.832 --> 00:13:29.872
&gt;&gt; It's really, really difficult.

00:13:29.872 --> 00:13:32.565
I can't understate how difficult it is.

00:13:34.015 --> 00:13:37.515
The guys that I went to for advice,

00:13:37.515 --> 00:13:42.465
that I looked up to when I
was starting Softcat and

00:13:42.465 --> 00:13:48.279
figuring out how do I get a short
only fund up and running?

00:13:48.279 --> 00:13:52.878
Those guys were retiring, and
not because they're old, but

00:13:52.878 --> 00:13:55.630
simply because it's intolerable.

00:13:55.630 --> 00:14:00.578
The losses that short sellers have
had to suffer have been intolerable.

00:14:00.578 --> 00:14:05.603
But it's not just that, it's sort of
this cycle where yes, the losses are bad

00:14:05.603 --> 00:14:10.475
because the markets are going up,
because there's a lot of liquidity, and

00:14:10.475 --> 00:14:15.067
because fundamentals have become
much more disconnected from price.

00:14:15.067 --> 00:14:18.686
But that also feeds into
the capital allocation cycle,

00:14:18.686 --> 00:14:21.920
where allocators are looking
at short sellers and

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:25.320
saying why am I going to pay
to be hedged in this market?

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:26.508
I'll just lose money.

00:14:26.508 --> 00:14:33.386
So there hasn't been much allocating
activity to short focus funds,

00:14:33.386 --> 00:14:38.174
so that has pushed us to
the brink of extinction.

00:14:38.174 --> 00:14:43.770
And it's just you can only tolerate so
much, right, as far as losses,

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:48.265
as far as willing to tolerate
those losses in the market.

00:14:48.265 --> 00:14:54.540
So many short sellers have just tried to
figure out different ways of doing things.

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:59.175
And everyone's heard
the phrase your bull market

00:14:59.175 --> 00:15:02.714
dies when the last bear capitulates.

00:15:02.714 --> 00:15:06.770
So I think that statement's
a little bit archaic,

00:15:06.770 --> 00:15:11.769
because maybe in prior market cycles,
there were no bears like

00:15:11.769 --> 00:15:16.127
me who have the pain tolerance
to continue doing this.

00:15:16.127 --> 00:15:20.766
Because throughout this entire period,
we've remained short only, and

00:15:20.766 --> 00:15:25.180
we've always remained exposed
to that risk in the market.

00:15:25.180 --> 00:15:28.237
And yeah, it's certainly not easy.

00:15:28.237 --> 00:15:30.740
I understand why people
don't want to do it.

00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:37.127
But when the clearing moments happens
in the market, whenever that is,

00:15:37.127 --> 00:15:42.867
whatever that looks like, it's people
like me that come to pick up the pieces,

00:15:42.867 --> 00:15:45.713
because we've already done the work.

00:15:45.713 --> 00:15:48.428
We understand these businesses, so

00:15:48.428 --> 00:15:52.978
we can help provide signals for
what's the most effective and

00:15:52.978 --> 00:15:59.030
the safest ways to allocate capital in
that new market order, whenever it comes.

00:16:01.686 --> 00:16:05.919
So perhaps, let's maybe shift gears a
little bit because I think there's another

00:16:05.919 --> 00:16:08.879
topic that also tends to come
up a lot here at Stanford.

00:16:08.879 --> 00:16:13.820
And that's the increasing importance of
private markets as a conduit of capital

00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:16.011
to the growing companies of today.

00:16:16.011 --> 00:16:20.751
So you now have this growing space where
you don't have the ability to maybe easily

00:16:20.751 --> 00:16:22.549
bet against these companies.

00:16:22.549 --> 00:16:27.462
How do you hold them accountable if most
of the capital being raised is now in

00:16:27.462 --> 00:16:31.525
a space where it's not easy to
put on a position against them?

00:16:31.525 --> 00:16:33.519
&gt;&gt; So I'm glad I'm not there in person for

00:16:33.519 --> 00:16:37.276
this question because I think I would
have had some tomatoes thrown at me.

00:16:37.276 --> 00:16:44.054
But I'd just like to ask you,
do private investors need protecting?

00:16:44.054 --> 00:16:46.585
Because I would say in most cases,

00:16:46.585 --> 00:16:51.484
public markets need to be protected
from private investor exits.

00:16:51.484 --> 00:16:58.445
These private equity and VC dudes,
they're not stupid, they are stupid rich.

00:16:58.445 --> 00:17:01.075
So in most cases in my experience,

00:17:01.075 --> 00:17:05.341
these investors know exactly
whether the technology or

00:17:05.341 --> 00:17:11.166
idea they're investing in is something
that's even remotely plausible.

00:17:11.166 --> 00:17:13.633
More often than not, they just don't care.

00:17:13.633 --> 00:17:19.647
[LAUGH] They'll happily invest in these
non-ideas that are completely fantastical.

00:17:19.647 --> 00:17:26.345
Because let's see, they get in these
businesses at ridiculous terms.

00:17:26.345 --> 00:17:32.721
Their investors give them more money to
do it and they'll pay them higher fees.

00:17:32.721 --> 00:17:36.162
So if you want to keep
getting more stupid rich,

00:17:36.162 --> 00:17:40.763
you need to invest in more stupider and
stupider businesses.

00:17:40.763 --> 00:17:46.850
Because as long as the equity markets are
preaching, buy the dip, look at that TAM,

00:17:46.850 --> 00:17:51.854
diamond hands, then private equity
investors will get their exit.

00:17:51.854 --> 00:17:54.544
So [LAUGH] what we've seen
over the past decade is

00:17:54.544 --> 00:17:57.573
that hedge fund managers
are looking at all of this and

00:17:57.573 --> 00:18:01.101
saying hey, we can't make money
in public markets anymore.

00:18:01.101 --> 00:18:03.233
So we're going to do that too.

00:18:03.233 --> 00:18:05.295
And it's just been toxic.

00:18:05.295 --> 00:18:09.935
Its toxic for public markets because
the pension funds, the retail investors

00:18:09.935 --> 00:18:14.732
who get duped into buying this trash,
they're the ones who are holding the bag.

00:18:14.732 --> 00:18:18.487
But it's also toxic for
the businesses themselves.

00:18:18.487 --> 00:18:21.943
I think a very good example of some,

00:18:21.943 --> 00:18:28.175
the toxic elements of this is what
you see happening in Tel Aviv.

00:18:28.175 --> 00:18:31.960
It's become now,
it's the most expensive city in the world.

00:18:31.960 --> 00:18:35.252
SoftBank has opened an office there.

00:18:35.252 --> 00:18:40.173
You have millions that are being thrown at
very smart young people who don't have any

00:18:40.173 --> 00:18:44.634
idea what they're going to build, or
what they're going to do with that money.

00:18:44.634 --> 00:18:49.881
And just ignoring the businesses
that might have come public,

00:18:49.881 --> 00:18:52.262
that might be sort of fraudy.

00:18:52.262 --> 00:18:57.559
There are startups that are good
ideas that are run by good people,

00:18:57.559 --> 00:19:01.531
that deserve a chance at
growing their business,

00:19:01.531 --> 00:19:06.557
that are being prematurely forced
to go public through SPACs.

00:19:06.557 --> 00:19:12.429
Because and often times at absurd
valuations that the founders

00:19:12.429 --> 00:19:17.747
never ever would have imagined,
and will never accept,

00:19:17.747 --> 00:19:22.535
except their VC backers
are egging them on to do it.

00:19:22.535 --> 00:19:26.850
So they're now entering public markets
when they're totally not prepared for it.

00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:31.083
So to me,
this is literally value destruction.

00:19:31.083 --> 00:19:32.894
It is not innovation.

00:19:32.894 --> 00:19:37.881
It's like the opposite side of
vulture capitalism, [LAUGH] but

00:19:37.881 --> 00:19:39.960
it's equally damaging.

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:44.273
So how do we solve this problem?

00:19:44.273 --> 00:19:49.078
Again, comes down to allocators,
allocators continuously pump

00:19:49.078 --> 00:19:53.300
money into private equity funds,
into venture capital.

00:19:53.300 --> 00:19:58.973
They hold the bag so
they should do more to hold these various

00:19:58.973 --> 00:20:04.772
firms accountable, and
that's I think the place to start.

00:20:04.772 --> 00:20:08.135
&gt;&gt; So I guess maybe what
follows from that then is,

00:20:08.135 --> 00:20:11.752
as a GP who's had
conversations with allocators,

00:20:11.752 --> 00:20:16.646
what question do you think they
ought to be asking that they aren't?

00:20:16.646 --> 00:20:20.761
That would help them maybe
figure out who's for real,

00:20:20.761 --> 00:20:23.989
and who's just chasing the latest trend?

00:20:23.989 --> 00:20:27.381
&gt;&gt; Well, they really should be thinking

00:20:27.381 --> 00:20:32.268
about what is the real thesis
behind this investment?

00:20:32.268 --> 00:20:38.160
Is my manager providing some
kind of story that isn't even

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:45.137
a solid story that they're using just
as like a veneer to cover up what

00:20:45.137 --> 00:20:50.329
they know is a guaranteed
money-making venture?

00:20:50.329 --> 00:20:54.614
The thing is with these
sorts of situations,

00:20:54.614 --> 00:20:59.933
regulators are watching,
politicians are watching.

00:20:59.933 --> 00:21:04.257
Increasingly, private equity
venture capital is no longer

00:21:04.257 --> 00:21:07.825
the darling innovation
driving Silicon Valley.

00:21:07.825 --> 00:21:11.011
People are starting to call out the fees,

00:21:11.011 --> 00:21:15.937
call out the way that they actually
measure their performance.

00:21:15.937 --> 00:21:19.948
They're calling out the effect that
they're having on these new developing

00:21:19.948 --> 00:21:20.696
businesses.

00:21:20.696 --> 00:21:23.896
So before someone's hand is forced,

00:21:23.896 --> 00:21:27.995
allocators should really
be more thoughtful and

00:21:27.995 --> 00:21:34.701
put more pressure on having these firms
actually explain what the business is.

00:21:34.701 --> 00:21:39.331
What is the plan for that business?

00:21:39.331 --> 00:21:44.253
And what is a realistic exit so
that those businesses will not just

00:21:44.253 --> 00:21:48.294
collapse after the venture
backing has been pulled?

00:21:51.793 --> 00:21:54.952
&gt;&gt; And so I think kind of branching
off of that, there's maybe,

00:21:54.952 --> 00:21:59.350
there's the allocator angle to look at and
then perhaps the regulator angle as well.

00:21:59.350 --> 00:22:03.786
And I think one question that's been
raised a little bit now is how the recent

00:22:03.786 --> 00:22:05.603
SEC focus on SPAC accounting.

00:22:05.603 --> 00:22:08.443
Restatements is going to
impact some of that behaviour.

00:22:08.443 --> 00:22:11.427
And sort of what's your take on that?

00:22:11.427 --> 00:22:14.142
&gt;&gt; I think it should have happened sooner.

00:22:14.142 --> 00:22:16.062
[LAUGH] It should have happened sooner.

00:22:16.062 --> 00:22:20.166
Because think about how many SPACs came
to market before the SEC came out and

00:22:20.166 --> 00:22:24.550
said that we need to take a look at the
disclosure requirements for these SPACs.

00:22:24.550 --> 00:22:30.637
We're always slow to the ball.

00:22:30.637 --> 00:22:32.782
Fraudsters are always way, fraudsters and

00:22:32.782 --> 00:22:35.575
greedy people are always going to
be many steps ahead of us.

00:22:35.575 --> 00:22:40.341
So I would think that given
the scrutiny that the SEC is now

00:22:40.341 --> 00:22:44.613
focusing upon SPACs and
just the risks involved and

00:22:44.613 --> 00:22:50.693
the fact that SPACs are wildly
underperforming in the market right now.

00:22:50.693 --> 00:22:55.735
I think businesses, business
founders will give some more thought

00:22:55.735 --> 00:23:00.164
to just going along with what
their VC partners are saying.

00:23:00.164 --> 00:23:05.865
And they'll probably hold off on
trying to come to market through SPAC.

00:23:05.865 --> 00:23:10.755
And we are actually seeing
this happen more where these

00:23:10.755 --> 00:23:15.019
mergers just blow up,
so that's a good thing.

00:23:15.019 --> 00:23:21.340
But the companies that are still pursuing
the SPACs are at this point in the cycle,

00:23:21.340 --> 00:23:25.565
I think are guaranteed to be garbage for
the most part.

00:23:25.565 --> 00:23:26.555
That's at least from what I've seen.

00:23:28.898 --> 00:23:33.082
&gt;&gt; And I think that's a sentiment that's
being echoed in a lot of places where

00:23:33.082 --> 00:23:36.814
the flow of SPAC capital was all but
dried up in more recent months.

00:23:36.814 --> 00:23:39.642
So I think maybe we can
shift gears a little bit,

00:23:39.642 --> 00:23:43.909
maybe talk about some of the ins and
outs of what you do as a short seller.

00:23:43.909 --> 00:23:49.143
I think one of the biggest challenges
that we hear about from the outside is

00:23:49.143 --> 00:23:54.462
that a lot of the executive teams,
management teams who you are interested

00:23:54.462 --> 00:23:58.535
in hearing from Once they know that
you're taking the short position,

00:23:58.535 --> 00:24:01.290
they know who you are,
they don't want to talk to you.

00:24:01.290 --> 00:24:04.009
How do you get the ear of somebody
who's kind of predisposed to

00:24:04.009 --> 00:24:05.030
see you as the bad guy?

00:24:06.670 --> 00:24:09.238
&gt;&gt; Well, I try first and foremost,

00:24:09.238 --> 00:24:14.070
to operate in a way that
is completely unassailable.

00:24:14.070 --> 00:24:21.242
So if the company's management was to
reject me, to look at me in the wrong way,

00:24:21.242 --> 00:24:26.059
to try and do something,
it should for the most part,

00:24:26.059 --> 00:24:31.580
make them look unreasonable and
make them look guilty.

00:24:31.580 --> 00:24:37.425
So even if the management itself
may view me as their adversary,

00:24:37.425 --> 00:24:43.055
I have been to events for example,
where are the shareholders

00:24:43.055 --> 00:24:48.270
of the company know who I am and
hold me in some regard.

00:24:48.270 --> 00:24:53.480
So if the company's executives
try to be hostile towards

00:24:53.480 --> 00:24:59.016
me in those in person events,
shareholders have intervened

00:24:59.016 --> 00:25:04.117
on my behalf because they
understand that I'm not there

00:25:04.117 --> 00:25:09.025
to cause a scene,
I'm not there to create any drama.

00:25:09.025 --> 00:25:14.261
I'm just there to observe and
I'm there to engage

00:25:14.261 --> 00:25:19.007
just as any other market
participant would.

00:25:19.007 --> 00:25:24.144
So in general, in general I have a good
track record of being able to sit down

00:25:24.144 --> 00:25:29.056
with management because just frankly
it's easier for them to do that.

00:25:29.056 --> 00:25:33.978
Because maybe there's a small chance
they can convince me that everything I'm

00:25:33.978 --> 00:25:36.924
asking about I'm just
completely misguided.

00:25:36.924 --> 00:25:42.710
So far hasn't happened [LAUGH] but
still they can at least try.

00:25:42.710 --> 00:25:47.514
But it's interesting because you often
hear other short sellers say that they

00:25:47.514 --> 00:25:52.940
don't speak to management because they
don't want to be seduced by the lies.

00:25:52.940 --> 00:25:57.440
I would like to tell you all that
I think that is complete BS,

00:25:57.440 --> 00:26:01.670
because if there's any very solidly,
consistently,

00:26:01.670 --> 00:26:06.200
BS detectors in the market,
they're short sellers.

00:26:06.200 --> 00:26:07.370
They're deeply cynical.

00:26:07.370 --> 00:26:11.635
It's going to be very hard for
any even the most charismatic of

00:26:11.635 --> 00:26:16.420
management to ever be convincing
them of any sort of lie.

00:26:16.420 --> 00:26:20.869
So I would think that most of the time
other short sellers say this,

00:26:20.869 --> 00:26:25.480
because they don't get the same
opportunity to meet with management

00:26:25.480 --> 00:26:28.277
because they operate in a different way.

00:26:28.277 --> 00:26:31.226
So they might have a much
more public presence,

00:26:31.226 --> 00:26:35.874
they might be publishing reports,
they might be frequently talking about

00:26:35.874 --> 00:26:40.540
the company on Twitter and like live
tweeting, meeting with management.

00:26:40.540 --> 00:26:44.486
So I stay away from all of that
because I see the value in

00:26:44.486 --> 00:26:48.975
actually being able to sit down and
have those discussions.

00:26:48.975 --> 00:26:53.345
If nothing else, if there's nothing
informational, it's really about,

00:26:53.345 --> 00:26:58.054
sometimes the softer things and being able
to assess behavior in those meetings and

00:26:58.054 --> 00:26:59.416
establish a baseline.

00:26:59.416 --> 00:27:02.098
How stressed is this executive?

00:27:02.098 --> 00:27:04.790
How much is he sweating
in these situations?

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:12.048
So I hope that through this, I'm able to
just sort of normalize having discussions,

00:27:12.048 --> 00:27:17.172
for company management to sit down
with those who have critical views and

00:27:17.172 --> 00:27:21.792
probing questions just as they
would with any analyst who wants to

00:27:21.792 --> 00:27:24.480
congratulate them on good earnings.

00:27:24.480 --> 00:27:29.529
It should be just a sign
of a healthy market, and

00:27:29.529 --> 00:27:34.335
the strength really of
the business itself.

00:27:36.576 --> 00:27:39.942
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think one of
the biggest examples that and

00:27:39.942 --> 00:27:43.938
I talked about it in the lead up to
this talk was your work on Wirecard.

00:27:43.938 --> 00:27:46.649
And sort of the how you were
able to get inside the tent and

00:27:46.649 --> 00:27:48.443
figure out what was really going on.

00:27:48.443 --> 00:27:52.296
So I think it would be great if you could
talk about that maybe a little bit.

00:27:52.296 --> 00:27:59.698
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so criticism of Wirecard
began the day there was the Wirecard.

00:27:59.698 --> 00:28:05.658
And it didn't start with short sellers, it
started with Wirecard's own shareholders.

00:28:05.658 --> 00:28:12.409
This was a business that was focused on
processing payments in the early days for

00:28:12.409 --> 00:28:17.261
online pornography, and
that's literally all it was.

00:28:17.261 --> 00:28:22.283
And in some ways,
that's literally all it ever was, if there

00:28:22.283 --> 00:28:28.563
was any legitimate part of Wirecards
business it was that it did it process for

00:28:28.563 --> 00:28:33.315
porn and process for
offshore gaming, things like this.

00:28:33.315 --> 00:28:37.510
This sort of gray market of
ecommerce Wirecard was there.

00:28:37.510 --> 00:28:42.698
So, yes,
that was the legitimate piece of Wirecard.

00:28:42.698 --> 00:28:45.909
But the thing with these
types of businesses,

00:28:45.909 --> 00:28:50.768
businesses that are focused on
facilitating transactions in the gray

00:28:50.768 --> 00:28:54.247
market digitally,
they're doomed to failure.

00:28:54.247 --> 00:29:00.588
So by and large they never go public,
because they don't want the scrutiny.

00:29:00.588 --> 00:29:03.588
And they never get very large.

00:29:03.588 --> 00:29:08.448
And that's just because they're limited by
just the economics of the business model.

00:29:08.448 --> 00:29:14.310
Because the more you have transactions
that are in these sketchy areas,

00:29:14.310 --> 00:29:18.457
the more likely that
a consumer will allege fraud.

00:29:18.457 --> 00:29:23.228
And if there's potential fraud and
the bank that issued that credit

00:29:23.228 --> 00:29:27.339
card agrees with the consumer,
then you're on the hook.

00:29:27.339 --> 00:29:30.194
And then you have to,
the fees start to add up.

00:29:30.194 --> 00:29:34.372
And eventually what happens is you
get kicked off the card networks.

00:29:34.372 --> 00:29:39.889
So this is, in a nutshell
what Wirecard's business was.

00:29:39.889 --> 00:29:45.298
Yet somehow Wirecard managed to
become a $20 billion company.

00:29:45.298 --> 00:29:51.723
It managed to send those shareholders
that ask those probing questions to jail.

00:29:51.723 --> 00:29:56.425
[LAUGH] And it also managed to time and

00:29:56.425 --> 00:30:01.128
time again, completely just brush

00:30:01.128 --> 00:30:05.687
aside short seller allegations.

00:30:05.687 --> 00:30:13.221
And this last round of criticism against
Wirecard, it also tried to brush off.

00:30:13.221 --> 00:30:21.442
So let's go back a few years to 2017.

00:30:21.442 --> 00:30:26.239
So at this time in 2017,
a lot of things were happening,

00:30:26.239 --> 00:30:29.450
especially within the payments space.

00:30:31.260 --> 00:30:36.248
If you don't know already Wirecard,
now people are admitting

00:30:36.248 --> 00:30:40.590
it was basically a front for
global money laundering.

00:30:42.730 --> 00:30:47.627
What you see happening in 2017
is that a lot of the business,

00:30:47.627 --> 00:30:52.072
a lot of that laundering
business that was flowing through

00:30:52.072 --> 00:30:56.630
Wirecard is finding a home in crypto and
in digital assets.

00:30:56.630 --> 00:31:01.629
And we actually see a lot of the
individuals that were bringing business

00:31:01.629 --> 00:31:05.830
to Wirecard now starting
crypto businesses instead.

00:31:05.830 --> 00:31:10.566
So Wirecard was fundamentally under
pressure because the whole thing was

00:31:10.566 --> 00:31:14.612
kept alive because they had
tremendous volume coming from that

00:31:14.612 --> 00:31:16.536
money laundering business.

00:31:16.536 --> 00:31:18.485
So what happens next?

00:31:18.485 --> 00:31:23.825
Germany decides Wirecard is one of
the best companies in our country,

00:31:23.825 --> 00:31:27.091
so we are going to include
them in the the DAX.

00:31:27.091 --> 00:31:32.603
So DAX think of like the German
version of a Dow Jones.

00:31:32.603 --> 00:31:38.312
So it's like the top, most solidly
blue chip companies in Germany and

00:31:38.312 --> 00:31:41.428
they we're going to include Wirecard.

00:31:41.428 --> 00:31:47.523
At the same time,
a whistleblower who worked for

00:31:47.523 --> 00:31:52.876
Wirecard in Asia came forward with fraud,

00:31:52.876 --> 00:31:57.310
fake contracts that he saw in.

00:31:57.310 --> 00:32:00.359
Wirecard Asia, and
he then took that information and

00:32:00.359 --> 00:32:02.062
went to the Financial Times.

00:32:02.062 --> 00:32:06.430
And the Financial Times started
publishing, on Wirecard.

00:32:06.430 --> 00:32:09.424
So what was Wirecard response?

00:32:09.424 --> 00:32:11.200
Fake News?

00:32:11.200 --> 00:32:13.147
Financial Times is criminal.

00:32:13.147 --> 00:32:15.280
It's colluding with short sellers.

00:32:15.280 --> 00:32:20.962
And what is the German securities
regulator say when one of the best

00:32:20.962 --> 00:32:28.181
financial news outlets decides to publish
all this critical work on this business.

00:32:28.181 --> 00:32:32.823
The German securities
regulator Boffin comes out and

00:32:32.823 --> 00:32:37.570
says we're going to investigate
the Financial Times for

00:32:37.570 --> 00:32:43.880
market manipulation, so, [LAUGH]
A few months later what happens next?

00:32:43.880 --> 00:32:48.878
Why are cars stock after getting
the DAX The stock price doesn't seem

00:32:48.878 --> 00:32:50.182
to go up anymore.

00:32:50.182 --> 00:32:51.747
This is a problem and also,

00:32:51.747 --> 00:32:56.452
the stock price also seems to go down when
the Financial Times publishes reports.

00:32:56.452 --> 00:33:00.503
So what does the German securities
regulator decide to do?

00:33:00.503 --> 00:33:04.788
They say we're going to ban
short selling in Wirecard.

00:33:04.788 --> 00:33:10.058
[LAUGH] So this was just an incredible
moment and I was actually at CASI for

00:33:10.058 --> 00:33:15.158
the first time within days of this
announcement and it was the first time

00:33:15.158 --> 00:33:20.513
you've ever seen this in any developed
market where a single security that

00:33:20.513 --> 00:33:26.393
isn't systemically important have short
selling ban it was absolutely insane.

00:33:26.393 --> 00:33:31.179
So we knew at that point despite our
efforts to get Boffin on board with

00:33:31.179 --> 00:33:34.989
our analysis that they
weren't going to do anything.

00:33:34.989 --> 00:33:39.836
So as a short seller it's really important
for us to focus on who is going to care

00:33:39.836 --> 00:33:45.323
about the information that we have and our
research was focused on money laundering.

00:33:45.323 --> 00:33:49.975
And money laundering matters to no
one more than the United States.

00:33:49.975 --> 00:33:50.969
So that was our angle.

00:33:50.969 --> 00:33:52.369
So we dug in deep.

00:33:52.369 --> 00:33:55.743
We tried to figure out everything
Wirecard was doing in the US.

00:33:55.743 --> 00:33:59.500
We tried to figure out all the actors
involved who was the kingpin of

00:33:59.500 --> 00:34:00.450
that business.

00:34:00.450 --> 00:34:05.362
And we try to get as much confidence
around that information tied up

00:34:05.362 --> 00:34:08.530
neatly in a bow and
give it to authorities.

00:34:08.530 --> 00:34:15.769
So that they could do that, the next
steps which would mean subpoenas so

00:34:15.769 --> 00:34:20.234
this eventually led to march 2020 where

00:34:20.234 --> 00:34:24.832
the kingpin of the US
business was arrested.

00:34:24.832 --> 00:34:31.072
And another a German former
Wirecard guy was also arrested.

00:34:31.072 --> 00:34:34.406
And this was again before
anyone really knew what was

00:34:34.406 --> 00:34:39.188
happening because no one was following
the US and what was happening here.

00:34:39.188 --> 00:34:44.142
But Wirecard's co founder, the supposed
Russian agent, Yan Morris he was,

00:34:44.142 --> 00:34:46.769
of course following what was happening,

00:34:46.769 --> 00:34:50.310
I mean this US guy who was
arrested with his best friend.

00:34:50.310 --> 00:34:55.781
So it was the second week of March,
it was at Yan Morris 40th birthday

00:34:55.781 --> 00:35:02.366
party he had a complete mental breakdown,
he drank two bottles of vodka by himself,

00:35:02.366 --> 00:35:07.585
rolling around the ground crying,
you know mumbling to himself.

00:35:07.585 --> 00:35:12.819
And two months later Wirecard
of course collapsed because

00:35:12.819 --> 00:35:18.049
that man Jan Morris was gone,
$2 billion were gone, and

00:35:18.049 --> 00:35:25.021
seller, I had just covered my Wirecard,
my last few shares that I had short.

00:35:25.021 --> 00:35:29.418
It was about two weeks ago,
because it was finally

00:35:29.418 --> 00:35:34.247
delisted officially, so
I'm no longer in the trade.

00:35:34.247 --> 00:35:40.378
&gt;&gt; I guess that's a phase where the book
is finally closed on the Wirecard.

00:35:40.378 --> 00:35:46.052
I know that you put out a paper on your
funds on your firm's website basically

00:35:46.052 --> 00:35:51.374
explaining your response to Bothans
decision and sort of a argument for

00:35:51.374 --> 00:35:55.823
your role as the short seller
in keeping the market honest.

00:35:55.823 --> 00:35:59.782
And I think I'm kind of curious,
what was Boffins response to that paper?

00:35:59.782 --> 00:36:04.225
Were they receptive to your argument?

00:36:04.225 --> 00:36:08.490
&gt;&gt; Well, they didn't ignore me.

00:36:08.490 --> 00:36:12.497
Before I published the letter, of course,

00:36:12.497 --> 00:36:18.036
I gave Boffin a chance to respond to me,
and sit down with me.

00:36:18.036 --> 00:36:23.041
What did they say they said no they
said no they didn't ignore my emails and

00:36:23.041 --> 00:36:26.686
ignore my letter they read my letter but
they said no.

00:36:26.686 --> 00:36:31.432
I reached out to them again after that I
have information I have analysis that I

00:36:31.432 --> 00:36:36.118
feel you should know about Wirecard and
they were like No, not interested.

00:36:36.118 --> 00:36:40.725
I was in Frankfort speaking
at a legal conference, and

00:36:40.725 --> 00:36:43.637
I was outside of Boffins offices.

00:36:43.637 --> 00:36:46.527
I called them I said I'm here.

00:36:46.527 --> 00:36:48.401
I'm outside your door.

00:36:48.401 --> 00:36:52.060
Can we meet, they said no.

00:36:52.060 --> 00:36:57.911
[LAUGH] So, just like I said,
I have this information that I tied up,

00:36:57.911 --> 00:37:04.497
put it on a silver platter tied it up and
both had to they were not interested.

00:37:04.497 --> 00:37:09.109
So we had to find the authorities that
were interested in that information.

00:37:09.109 --> 00:37:12.725
&gt;&gt; You kind of have yourself in this
position where you really have to go over

00:37:12.725 --> 00:37:15.690
their heads for
somebody to start paying attention to it.

00:37:15.690 --> 00:37:18.189
&gt;&gt; Yeah!
I never want to do that,

00:37:18.189 --> 00:37:21.415
it's a German company, right?

00:37:21.415 --> 00:37:24.913
They are the domestic
regulator like I said,

00:37:24.913 --> 00:37:29.895
I don't like to create drama, so
I didn't want this to happen.

00:37:29.895 --> 00:37:32.180
I gave them every chance in the world, but

00:37:32.180 --> 00:37:35.262
it wasn't even like I wasn't
getting through to them.

00:37:35.262 --> 00:37:42.042
It's just that they were just saying no,
they wanted nothing to do with me.

00:37:42.042 --> 00:37:46.376
&gt;&gt; Perhaps it's an issue of alignment of
interests if it's your country's darling

00:37:46.376 --> 00:37:50.709
company that's driving your stock market
you maybe don't want to be the person who

00:37:50.709 --> 00:37:53.189
allows people to put in
that's against them.

00:37:53.189 --> 00:37:57.897
&gt;&gt; Well yeah because Germany is largely,
it's an industrial

00:37:57.897 --> 00:38:03.170
manufacturer type of economy, so
this was a technology company or

00:38:03.170 --> 00:38:07.517
at least it was able to go
around parading itself as so.

00:38:07.517 --> 00:38:11.453
So it was within German interest
to of course promote that,

00:38:11.453 --> 00:38:16.583
even if they didn't necessarily know
the criminal extent of the behaviors.

00:38:16.583 --> 00:38:20.645
That said,
perhaps between the US and Germany,

00:38:20.645 --> 00:38:25.614
there's a misalignment of
geopolitical interests here.

00:38:25.614 --> 00:38:29.640
Because the US, of course, is a lot
more interested in money laundering.

00:38:29.640 --> 00:38:34.497
They're a lot more interested in
going up against these sanctions

00:38:34.497 --> 00:38:36.240
on Russian oligarchs.

00:38:36.240 --> 00:38:39.604
That is something that is
a geopolitical interest for

00:38:39.604 --> 00:38:42.830
us also because we
are the world's fiat currency.

00:38:42.830 --> 00:38:50.253
So we do need to worry about the integrity
of the dollar and of US financial system.

00:38:50.253 --> 00:38:55.905
So money laundering is a big deal for
us, Germany [LAUGH] It's a dime a dozen.

00:38:55.905 --> 00:38:58.929
How many money laundering
scandals have been there?

00:38:58.929 --> 00:39:03.607
And I don't think this means that
no we can just say Germany is

00:39:03.607 --> 00:39:08.011
a money laundering haven but
it's just not a priority for

00:39:08.011 --> 00:39:13.814
them naturally just as much as trying
to diversify their economy, maybe.

00:39:13.814 --> 00:39:17.121
&gt;&gt; It's interesting that,
he fast forward to today and

00:39:17.121 --> 00:39:21.245
we look at what's going on in
Ukraine with the prices with Russia.

00:39:21.245 --> 00:39:25.730
One of the potential, I guess,
penalties that has been talked about,

00:39:25.730 --> 00:39:29.187
is limiting or
cutting off access to the SWIFT system.

00:39:29.187 --> 00:39:31.184
And so there are rumblings that Russia and

00:39:31.184 --> 00:39:34.219
China are looking to create
an international payment system.

00:39:34.219 --> 00:39:39.101
What does that do if suddenly you have
a section of global economy that is cut

00:39:39.101 --> 00:39:42.975
off from us control and
influence that's sort of a check and

00:39:42.975 --> 00:39:47.519
balance she's removed
&gt;&gt; Well just it's so

00:39:47.519 --> 00:39:52.292
interesting though that we're at
this moment because all of Scholtz

00:39:52.292 --> 00:39:56.610
the new chancellor of Germany
was in charge you know right

00:39:56.610 --> 00:40:01.815
Overseeing financial
regulations under Merkel.

00:40:01.815 --> 00:40:03.859
And that was during the Wirecard scandal.

00:40:03.859 --> 00:40:06.763
So he was in charge of overseeing
Boffin during that whole period.

00:40:06.763 --> 00:40:11.131
And that isn't to say that he
was guilty of anything, but

00:40:11.131 --> 00:40:14.044
it is just a severe lack of oversight.

00:40:14.044 --> 00:40:18.533
[LAUGH] So I think it's all very
interesting to see how this

00:40:18.533 --> 00:40:21.630
has all been playing out politically.

00:40:21.630 --> 00:40:27.207
But we already have an economy
that exists outside

00:40:27.207 --> 00:40:34.920
of the financial market that the US
oversees and regulates, right?

00:40:34.920 --> 00:40:39.421
The thing is, these criminals,
organized crime,

00:40:39.421 --> 00:40:44.638
have found a way to make that
market bigger and more liquid and

00:40:44.638 --> 00:40:48.749
more convenient for
them through innovation.

00:40:48.749 --> 00:40:53.364
And the government,
just as it is with with most things,

00:40:53.364 --> 00:40:56.356
is very slow on the innovation side.

00:40:56.356 --> 00:41:01.466
And it's going to be a really
hard game of catching

00:41:01.466 --> 00:41:06.447
up now to all of these
new innovations in DeFi,

00:41:06.447 --> 00:41:09.640
Web3, all of these things,

00:41:09.640 --> 00:41:15.551
while it's like these
criminals are printing money.

00:41:15.551 --> 00:41:18.277
[LAUGH] That's effectively what it is.

00:41:18.277 --> 00:41:22.290
So rogue states, more oppressive regimes,

00:41:22.290 --> 00:41:27.464
they might see a benefit in
aligning themselves with these

00:41:27.464 --> 00:41:35.084
criminal interests because it suits some
of their own national priorities, right?

00:41:35.084 --> 00:41:41.704
So it's going to be difficult, but I think
we need to act sooner rather than later.

00:41:41.704 --> 00:41:45.741
And this isn't to say that
we cut them out of SWIFT.

00:41:45.741 --> 00:41:53.329
I think it's probably more difficult and
not as practical of a thing.

00:41:53.329 --> 00:41:57.888
I'm not sure how it would work,
but just thinking about

00:41:57.888 --> 00:42:02.643
where is the dark money,
how is that currently existing?

00:42:02.643 --> 00:42:09.171
That economy, how do we do
a better job of regulating it?

00:42:09.171 --> 00:42:13.209
Or at least integrating
our own economic system,

00:42:13.209 --> 00:42:18.027
our own financial systems,
in a more cohesive sort of way.

00:42:18.027 --> 00:42:22.496
&gt;&gt; Right, I guess, it's how do we get a
handle on these flows of payments where as

00:42:22.496 --> 00:42:26.493
with many other things, technology
seems to move a mile a minute, and

00:42:26.493 --> 00:42:30.317
government regulation can hardly
keep up with the pace of change.

00:42:30.317 --> 00:42:33.380
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but
human beings are also prone to failure.

00:42:33.380 --> 00:42:38.721
I'm sure everyone has read
about the great Bitcoin heist.

00:42:38.721 --> 00:42:40.904
How did they eventually get caught?

00:42:40.904 --> 00:42:44.529
It was because they put
money onto a prepaid card.

00:42:44.529 --> 00:42:50.365
And that's also kind of
what led us to Wirecard.

00:42:50.365 --> 00:42:55.415
Because eventually, you will need to
be able to use cash, and prepaid cards,

00:42:55.415 --> 00:42:59.792
they're very old school but
they're also still very convenient.

00:42:59.792 --> 00:43:04.229
But these are little things,
prepaid cards are not a new innovation.

00:43:04.229 --> 00:43:09.689
And ultimately, people who
are needing to have clean money,

00:43:09.689 --> 00:43:12.741
they sometimes want actual cash.

00:43:12.741 --> 00:43:16.146
So if they need to use a prepaid card for
that, then maybe

00:43:16.146 --> 00:43:20.937
we need to start thinking about why do we
have prepaid cards in the first place.

00:43:20.937 --> 00:43:23.227
&gt;&gt; Right, exactly.

00:43:23.227 --> 00:43:27.133
So I think, as we're thinking about
situations like Wirecard, you've

00:43:27.133 --> 00:43:31.488
got a couple different players who are
expected to sort of keep everybody honest.

00:43:31.488 --> 00:43:34.712
You've got the government,
you've got the investors such as yourself,

00:43:34.712 --> 00:43:38.003
who are looking at the company and
trying to ferret out what's going wrong.

00:43:38.003 --> 00:43:42.012
And of course, you've got the auditors
who are supposed to vet the financial

00:43:42.012 --> 00:43:43.438
statements for accuracy.

00:43:43.438 --> 00:43:46.985
I think one question that kind of
pops up as we look at a situation is,

00:43:46.985 --> 00:43:51.601
why wouldn't the auditors have identified
these items perhaps before short-sellers

00:43:51.601 --> 00:43:55.775
would have caught on to them, given that
they were essentially responsible for

00:43:55.775 --> 00:43:58.172
ensuring these were accurately presented?

00:43:58.172 --> 00:44:01.793
&gt;&gt; Wirecard, I think,

00:44:01.793 --> 00:44:05.823
is a unique situation.

00:44:05.823 --> 00:44:10.934
It is a very clear example of what we

00:44:10.934 --> 00:44:16.753
would call an overly cozy relationship

00:44:16.753 --> 00:44:21.868
between the auditing partners and

00:44:21.868 --> 00:44:27.706
their client, Wirecard in this case.

00:44:27.706 --> 00:44:32.566
When you're auditing someone for
such a long period of time, you start

00:44:32.566 --> 00:44:37.845
asking fewer and fewer questions, or
you take certain things at face value.

00:44:37.845 --> 00:44:43.309
So I think we have to consider what is
the right period of time that an auditor

00:44:43.309 --> 00:44:48.866
should be auditing a particular business
and balancing their expertise and

00:44:48.866 --> 00:44:53.987
familiarity of the financial statements
with the fact that they are so

00:44:53.987 --> 00:44:57.933
closely working with the same client for
many years.

00:44:57.933 --> 00:45:01.167
So that's one issue.

00:45:01.167 --> 00:45:04.916
But we can't just depend on one piece,

00:45:04.916 --> 00:45:10.711
one stakeholder within this
ecosystem to find everything,

00:45:10.711 --> 00:45:15.155
because auditors can get screwed over,
too.

00:45:15.155 --> 00:45:20.300
Because, when we're talking about frauds,
when we're talking about businesses

00:45:20.300 --> 00:45:24.793
criminal management, sociopathic
management, they're going to lie and

00:45:24.793 --> 00:45:29.867
say whatever they need to, create whatever
fake documentation that they need to,

00:45:29.867 --> 00:45:32.195
to defraud that the auditors as well.

00:45:32.195 --> 00:45:33.295
So we can't forget that piece.

00:45:33.295 --> 00:45:38.406
So there needs to be multiple checks and
balances here because it's

00:45:38.406 --> 00:45:43.619
not as though short-sellers
are the first line of defense in fraud.

00:45:43.619 --> 00:45:50.063
We're basically one of the last
lines of defense against fraud.

00:45:50.063 --> 00:45:53.954
So yeah, I can't just put all
the blame on the auditors.

00:45:53.954 --> 00:45:59.191
And at the end of the day,
auditors are hired by the company itself.

00:45:59.191 --> 00:46:02.287
And they're not there to find fraud,

00:46:02.287 --> 00:46:06.932
they're there to just ensure
all the boxes are checked,

00:46:06.932 --> 00:46:12.760
that the books are balanced, and
that's really the crux of their job.

00:46:12.760 --> 00:46:17.637
What I don't think auditors,
maybe they could perhaps spend

00:46:17.637 --> 00:46:21.670
more time assessing some
of the softer features and

00:46:21.670 --> 00:46:27.890
more governance-related features within
a business, like tone at the top.

00:46:27.890 --> 00:46:32.808
I think it's really important to
understand the culture of the place

00:46:32.808 --> 00:46:35.871
because fraud doesn't occur in a vacuum.

00:46:35.871 --> 00:46:40.357
There's usually opportunities,
incentives that exist there for

00:46:40.357 --> 00:46:44.212
someone to potentially put
their livelihood on the line to

00:46:44.212 --> 00:46:47.214
commit fraud that will
enrich the business.

00:46:47.214 --> 00:46:51.849
So yeah, there are small
things that can be done, but

00:46:51.849 --> 00:46:56.594
even still,
it'll never end with just the auditors.

00:46:56.594 --> 00:47:02.006
I think every piece of
this is equally important.

00:47:02.006 --> 00:47:05.520
&gt;&gt; I think that tone from the top
piece is really one that does or

00:47:05.520 --> 00:47:08.895
ought to resonate heavily with
the people on this call who

00:47:08.895 --> 00:47:13.327
are from the Stanford Business School and
other business school classes,

00:47:13.327 --> 00:47:17.756
who may well be going on to these sorts
of leadership positions at some point

00:47:17.756 --> 00:47:21.657
in our career and will be in
the position to set that sort of a tone.

00:47:21.657 --> 00:47:26.852
And basically,
it's that the trouble begins long before

00:47:26.852 --> 00:47:33.118
it gets to deciding which numbers to
adjust or which figures to fudge.

00:47:33.118 --> 00:47:36.493
It really starts with how
people think about right and

00:47:36.493 --> 00:47:40.023
wrong from the culture of the company,
it sounds like.

00:47:40.023 --> 00:47:43.548
&gt;&gt; Yes, exactly, and
sometimes it's just as practical as,

00:47:43.548 --> 00:47:45.175
what can you get away with?

00:47:45.175 --> 00:47:50.293
Because not everyone is going to have
the most sterling moral compass.

00:47:50.293 --> 00:47:55.041
So with Wirecard, for example,
They were literally taking plastic

00:47:55.041 --> 00:47:59.262
bags full of cash to and
from Wirecard headquarters, right?

00:47:59.262 --> 00:48:04.424
And Wirecard employees, everyone
this is in full, plain sight, right?

00:48:04.424 --> 00:48:10.061
So if I'm some middle manager in Asia and
I hear that this is going on and

00:48:10.061 --> 00:48:14.362
I realize that I can be as
aggressive as possible, and

00:48:14.362 --> 00:48:20.216
I'll still have a job, and
I'll actually be rewarded by the business.

00:48:20.216 --> 00:48:24.166
&gt;&gt; My God, that sounds like something out
of a movie when you describe it that way.

00:48:24.166 --> 00:48:28.286
It's like a scene from Wolf of
Wall Street, I don't know [LAUGH].

00:48:28.286 --> 00:48:32.089
&gt;&gt; It's worse, it's even worse.

00:48:32.089 --> 00:48:37.019
&gt;&gt; So I guess as we're getting down
to some of our final questions here,

00:48:37.019 --> 00:48:40.929
I think one other item that
comes up from our audience,

00:48:40.929 --> 00:48:43.740
within the GameStop saga of last year.

00:48:43.740 --> 00:48:49.663
And sort of this narrative-driven
short selling and short squeezing.

00:48:49.663 --> 00:48:53.209
Melvin Capital almost went under because
of their short position on GameStop.

00:48:53.209 --> 00:48:58.009
And I think there was another fund that
made money on the opposite side of that

00:48:58.009 --> 00:48:59.218
trade going long.

00:48:59.218 --> 00:49:03.524
And I think that the question that comes
up a lot from retail investors and

00:49:03.524 --> 00:49:08.042
people who are kind of watching the market
is might there have been any sort of

00:49:08.042 --> 00:49:10.317
manipulation that had been going on?

00:49:10.317 --> 00:49:17.205
Are there bad actors, perhaps who are
getting involved in the midst of all this.

00:49:17.205 --> 00:49:19.100
&gt;&gt; There are bad actors everywhere.

00:49:19.100 --> 00:49:24.482
There are bad actors regardless
of market conditions.

00:49:24.482 --> 00:49:29.955
And I think,
just the difficulty of generating alpha in

00:49:29.955 --> 00:49:35.080
these markets have probably
forced some managers,

00:49:35.080 --> 00:49:42.419
who previously were not overly aggressive
or taking excessive leverage or

00:49:42.419 --> 00:49:47.094
engage in any kind of
dubious market activity.

00:49:47.094 --> 00:49:50.486
Perhaps they felt the pressure
too just because it's so

00:49:50.486 --> 00:49:53.093
hard to prove yourself to your investors.

00:49:53.093 --> 00:49:58.008
Or it's hard to keep your track
record going because once you start

00:49:58.008 --> 00:50:03.034
making a lot of money, people
are going to continually expect you to.

00:50:03.034 --> 00:50:07.517
Whether around GameStop,
there was a manipulation,

00:50:07.517 --> 00:50:11.811
I mean, it's beyond my expertise,
I have no idea.

00:50:11.811 --> 00:50:16.835
As a portfolio manager,
would I ever put myself in a position

00:50:16.835 --> 00:50:22.660
where in a single stock bet, long or
short, my business can go under.

00:50:22.660 --> 00:50:27.925
I would never do that, but
that's me and I'm a short seller with

00:50:27.925 --> 00:50:33.400
a perverse relationship with risk and
I'm saying this, so yeah.

00:50:33.400 --> 00:50:38.972
I don't understand why a PM would
let their book get to that point.

00:50:38.972 --> 00:50:44.281
But things happen,
funds blow up, people move on.

00:50:44.281 --> 00:50:48.403
It's not necessarily conspiracy,
it's the jungle.

00:50:48.403 --> 00:50:53.293
[LAUGH] This is how it goes.

00:50:53.293 --> 00:50:57.119
&gt;&gt; It's just the nature of
playing the game so to speak.

00:50:57.119 --> 00:50:58.155
&gt;&gt; Well, that's the thing.

00:50:58.155 --> 00:51:03.925
This market has been all about
playing games and not about thinking.

00:51:03.925 --> 00:51:11.765
&gt;&gt; So I think, as we're sort of getting
down to the end of our time here.

00:51:11.765 --> 00:51:16.693
I'm just kind of curious to hear a little
bit more about what it's like to be

00:51:16.693 --> 00:51:21.250
fun in career and sort of what does
the best day in your life look like?

00:51:21.250 --> 00:51:24.602
And maybe,
what does the worst day look like?

00:51:24.602 --> 00:51:31.174
&gt;&gt; Yeah, the best days are the ones
where that nagging question that I have,

00:51:31.174 --> 00:51:38.290
that I've been obsessing over for like
a year, I'm finally able to answer it.

00:51:38.290 --> 00:51:40.240
However that may be.

00:51:40.240 --> 00:51:44.859
And those days are only
rivaled by the days when I

00:51:44.859 --> 00:51:49.605
finally see my thesis
materializing before me.

00:51:49.605 --> 00:51:54.735
When a company comes out during their
earnings and totally kitchen sinks it.

00:51:54.735 --> 00:51:59.752
And when authorities come out and
say that they've made arrests of

00:51:59.752 --> 00:52:04.689
key figures that I suspected were
engaged in criminal activity.

00:52:04.689 --> 00:52:07.889
Or it's when the FTC sends a cease and

00:52:07.889 --> 00:52:13.891
desist order to a business that we
believe was preying on consumers.

00:52:13.891 --> 00:52:17.974
So when things like that happen,
those are the most satisfying.

00:52:17.974 --> 00:52:20.602
Those are the most rewarding days.

00:52:20.602 --> 00:52:24.222
The worst days [LAUGH] are days
like this one, believe it or not.

00:52:24.222 --> 00:52:27.554
It's just days where
the markets are bleeding.

00:52:27.554 --> 00:52:32.633
Where the market's sell off broadly,
violently, because they make my

00:52:32.633 --> 00:52:37.722
job as a PM very difficult, because
I can't rely on my trusted toolkit.

00:52:37.722 --> 00:52:39.803
I can't just look at my thesis,

00:52:39.803 --> 00:52:43.824
I can't look at my information,
my analysis, my research.

00:52:43.824 --> 00:52:50.154
I have to start making decisions based on
things that to me feel more speculative.

00:52:50.154 --> 00:52:55.292
So basically,
how much further down can something go?

00:52:55.292 --> 00:52:59.721
And how much risk I'm I
willing to leave on the table?

00:52:59.721 --> 00:53:05.321
So every day, I think that,
I incrementally get better at this and

00:53:05.321 --> 00:53:10.623
there's no better test than
the one the market we've been in.

00:53:10.623 --> 00:53:16.936
But it's really hard,
as someone who's so research driven.

00:53:16.936 --> 00:53:21.734
If fundamental factors remain unchanged,
justifying why I

00:53:21.734 --> 00:53:26.635
should change my positioning,
[LAUGH] it's very difficult.

00:53:26.635 --> 00:53:30.309
&gt;&gt; So
how does a day like that typically unfold?

00:53:30.309 --> 00:53:32.258
What's going through your head [LAUGH]?

00:53:32.258 --> 00:53:36.446
&gt;&gt; I guess I do talk to CASI because I
feel like every time I've done a CASI

00:53:36.446 --> 00:53:39.234
talk, it's been a bleeding
day in the markets.

00:53:39.234 --> 00:53:43.871
So yeah, I just,
I don't try to get too reflexive.

00:53:43.871 --> 00:53:47.350
I just take a step back,
focus on my research.

00:53:47.350 --> 00:53:51.952
And get a good night's rest and then
decide what I'm going to do the next day,

00:53:51.952 --> 00:53:53.647
no one's going to die [LAUGH].

00:53:53.647 --> 00:53:56.860
&gt;&gt; What about some of these things like
this New York times article that came out

00:53:56.860 --> 00:53:57.370
recently.

00:53:57.370 --> 00:53:58.911
Where I think you mentioned that,

00:53:58.911 --> 00:54:02.471
I think your printer turned on one night
and started printing out whistleblower

00:54:02.471 --> 00:54:03.077
letters-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:54:03.077 --> 00:54:06.133
&gt;&gt; Just on its own without
any input from you and

00:54:06.133 --> 00:54:11.014
that was almost like somebody hacked
in and they knew how to get at you.

00:54:11.014 --> 00:54:16.071
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we deal with this kind of stuff
all the time, it's like a running joke.

00:54:16.071 --> 00:54:21.671
Whenever something bad happens,
we'll attribute it to Wirecard or

00:54:21.671 --> 00:54:27.090
some other criminal interest that
we might be on the wrong side of.

00:54:27.090 --> 00:54:29.922
But you get used to it.

00:54:29.922 --> 00:54:33.704
I think in some ways, I've learned
to embrace it because it's probably

00:54:33.704 --> 00:54:37.432
validation that we're on the right
track with whatever we're doing.

00:54:37.432 --> 00:54:41.488
So it never gives me pause.

00:54:41.488 --> 00:54:45.521
When I got into this business, of course,
I knew it was going to be a dangerous

00:54:45.521 --> 00:54:48.284
business, that I know how
dangerous it could get.

00:54:48.284 --> 00:54:49.191
Absolutely not.

00:54:49.191 --> 00:54:55.128
But one of the main learnings is that
you can never be paranoid enough.

00:54:55.128 --> 00:54:56.943
So I'm always cautious.

00:54:56.943 --> 00:55:00.252
I'm always observant.

00:55:00.252 --> 00:55:04.221
But now, I'm in London, right?

00:55:04.221 --> 00:55:06.592
There was a time when I was nervous,

00:55:06.592 --> 00:55:11.187
maybe I couldn't do this kind of
work in London, but now I'm all in.

00:55:11.187 --> 00:55:14.627
[LAUGH] So I think here,
London is one of those places,

00:55:14.627 --> 00:55:18.538
parts of the city are basically
owned by Russian oligarchs.

00:55:18.538 --> 00:55:20.400
It's a very interesting experience.

00:55:20.400 --> 00:55:26.663
So it's an adrenaline rush
to be in the lion's den.

00:55:26.663 --> 00:55:31.685
&gt;&gt; I mean, did you feel like London is
sort of, I guess, the best place to be and

00:55:31.685 --> 00:55:37.337
to have a bird's eye view across all the
global markets rather than the US-centric,

00:55:37.337 --> 00:55:39.639
perhaps you might get in New York.

00:55:39.639 --> 00:55:44.550
&gt;&gt; Yes, exactly, you're basically smack
down in the middle and it's a gateway.

00:55:44.550 --> 00:55:48.519
Right for
all of the money laundering activity,

00:55:48.519 --> 00:55:52.111
London is the gateway
to the United States.

00:55:52.111 --> 00:55:57.110
And I think that it's really
fascinating to see play out because

00:55:57.110 --> 00:56:02.202
in some ways you know how Miami
could be considered the gateway for

00:56:02.202 --> 00:56:04.621
Latin America to get to the US.

00:56:04.621 --> 00:56:10.181
London is the same for
all of Europe and also Asia.

00:56:10.181 --> 00:56:12.531
So it's a really great place to be.

00:56:14.750 --> 00:56:19.372
This is the birthplace of a lot
of FinTech businesses, so

00:56:19.372 --> 00:56:22.370
I get to see it all happen firsthand.

00:56:24.900 --> 00:56:29.886
&gt;&gt; So, I think you've got a couple minutes
left here and I think what could be

00:56:29.886 --> 00:56:35.036
interesting to talk about is, is there
anything that you're seeing lately

00:56:35.036 --> 00:56:40.362
that perhaps you're not getting asked
about as much as you wish that you were?

00:56:42.720 --> 00:56:44.260
&gt;&gt; About anything?

00:56:44.260 --> 00:56:49.879
Honestly, people don't really
seem very concerned whether or

00:56:49.879 --> 00:56:56.655
not there's actually deep levels of
fraud and criminality at any business.

00:56:56.655 --> 00:57:00.440
That's the thing very broadly no
one wants to get in the weeds.

00:57:00.440 --> 00:57:03.560
No one wants the expert view.

00:57:03.560 --> 00:57:06.730
Everyone wants like the one liner.

00:57:06.730 --> 00:57:13.026
They want very superficial analysis,
very one dimensional analysis.

00:57:13.026 --> 00:57:17.727
No one really wants to hear
about all the work that

00:57:17.727 --> 00:57:22.554
goes into the businesses
that were researching.

00:57:22.554 --> 00:57:25.172
I think also because they don't care.

00:57:25.172 --> 00:57:30.367
And people will only care after
sentiment in the market shifts and

00:57:30.367 --> 00:57:33.395
we haven't gotten to that point yet.

00:57:33.395 --> 00:57:38.153
&gt;&gt; Do you think that that's going to
change in a year's time perhaps if things

00:57:38.153 --> 00:57:43.316
get tougher or is this just how things are
set to be just is it just human nature?

00:57:43.316 --> 00:57:46.944
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean cycles will
play out as they play out and

00:57:46.944 --> 00:57:50.835
some people will get lucky and
calling it the right way.

00:57:50.835 --> 00:57:57.578
I think we all know how this ends,
I just I have no idea when that happens.

00:57:57.578 --> 00:58:04.185
But it doesn't really matter for the
strategy because we're just always short,

00:58:04.185 --> 00:58:08.782
we're always mostly 100%
short the markets through

00:58:08.782 --> 00:58:13.103
the various companies that
we're betting against.

00:58:13.103 --> 00:58:17.454
So, whether there's mass calamity,
market apocalypse,

00:58:17.454 --> 00:58:22.400
whatever it may be, we are just here
being patient, continuing to do

00:58:22.400 --> 00:58:27.630
the old school old fashioned kind of
work that we've always been doing.

00:58:28.900 --> 00:58:34.450
&gt;&gt; It's true scuttle but
diligence and really digging in.

00:58:34.450 --> 00:58:35.410
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:58:35.410 --> 00:58:36.630
&gt;&gt; I want to thank you.

00:58:36.630 --> 00:58:40.772
Thank you so much for joining us for me
and for everybody who has been on the line

00:58:40.772 --> 00:58:43.850
with us, and hope everyone has
a great rest of their day.

00:58:43.850 --> 00:58:53.850
[MUSIC]

